          "field": "description",
          "page_id": "page-ad69ccc0ba93d99d82255fcc",
          "qa_findings": [],
          "review_status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "segment_id": "seg-d0df07843f75d2554e02b269",
          "segment_index": 13,
          "source_segment_id": "seg-d0df07843f75d2554e02b269",
          "source_text_sha256": "d235141b1e174b9f042ccd7789ba28556c646b5190e7a71af2be5b927a6e1c0c",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Description: On October 19, 2014, around 8:30 PM, a witness and his brother-in-law observed the silent passage of a dark triangular shape with red lights at each end. This phenomenon passed over the garden. The three points moved in a straight line, then formed a dark triangle. The witnesses left the observation site. Only one testimony was collected. Given the objective elements defined in the investigation report (see attached document), namely:\n\n- The possibility that the PAN is not a single, solid object but rather three independent objects.\n- The straight trajectory, like that of an object pushed by a steady and regular wind.\n- The red color, similar to that of lanterns.\n- The absence of noise.\n- The observation duration compatible with the lifespan of a lantern.\n- The observation date conducive to lantern releases, on weekends, for holidays, weddings, etc...\n\nConfusion with a group of three Thai lanterns is possible. However, we lack the azimuthal data for the movement of the PAN. This testimony is of low consistency: it is imprecise and lacks essential data for analysis (direction of movement), coming from a single witness without a photo. The observation is not particularly strange as the objects have a mundane behavior and appearance. The GEIPAN classifies this case as Category C due to lack of information and cross-references.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "273c3a3762385c2bf25b64ec534a2f55d9c198fe2b6fde6931dcb3569f4d678d"
        }
      ],
